.TH OPTIMTEST 1
.SH NAME
optimtest \- check optim(2) against answers known in advance
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B optimtest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Optimtest
checks
.IR optim (2)
and prints
.B OK
if everything agrees.
.B -v
prints each measured number.
.PP
Every problem here has a known answer: sqrt(2) is a root of
.IR x \u\s-22\s+2\d\-2,
0.7390851332151607 is the root of cos(\fIx\fP)\-\fIx\fP,
.RI ( x \-1)( x \-2)( x \-3)
has its three, Rosenbrock's function has its only minimum at (1,1) with
value 0, Powell's quartic has its at the origin, and the linear
programme every textbook opens with is worth 36 at (2,6).
.SS What the tests are for, one at a time
Several checks exist because a deliberately broken version passed
without them.
.PP
.B Brent
.B against
.BR bisection .
The answer alone cannot tell them apart, since both converge.
The function calls can: ten against fifty-one.
.PP
.B Newton
.B where
.B it
.BR fails .
On atan started from 2 it is thrown to 7e168 and then divides by a slope
that has underflowed to zero, and it must say so rather than return a
number.
.B Root
finds the same function's root without difficulty.
That contrast is why both exist.
.PP
.B Powell's
.BR quartic .
It is the case that needs Nelder-Mead's shrink step: reflection,
extension and contraction all fail near its minimum, and without
shrinking the simplex the method reaches the neighbourhood and crawls
forever \- 40000 evaluations without converging, against 2200 with it.
The value is small either way, so only the iteration count and the
converged flag can tell.
.PP
.B A
.B fit
.B from
.B a
.B long
.B way
.BR off .
A Gauss-Newton step from there overshoots, and what saves it is the
damping being increased when a step makes things worse.
Without that the fit stops after two iterations with a residual of 143
instead of 3e\-30.
What is checked is the residual and the fitted curve, not the
parameters: sin(\-1.3\fIx\fP+2.7416) is the same curve as
sin(1.3\fIx\fP+0.4) and the fit is entitled to find either.
.PP
.B Beale's
.BR example .
The classic linear programme on which a simplex that always takes the
steepest column cycles forever, returning to a tableau it has seen.
Under Bland's rule it finishes in a handful of pivots.
Nothing else in this program can tell the two rules apart.
.PP
.B A
.B local
.BR minimum .
From one start the curve fit settles into a different minimum than the
one the data came from, and reports that it converged \- because it has.
That is checked as
.I expected
behaviour, with a guard that fails if that start ever stops finding a
local minimum, since the check would then say nothing.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/optimtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR optim (2),
.IR stats (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each disagreement prints a line beginning
.B FAIL
naming what was measured, what came out and what was wanted; the count
follows and the program raises an exception.
.SH BUGS
Nothing here distinguishes central from one-sided differences in
.BR fit ;
the reason is in
.IR optim (2)'s
BUGS and is a property of the problems, not an oversight that a better
test would fix.
.PP
The linear programmes are tiny \- at most four variables and three
constraints.
Nothing checks a problem large enough for the accumulated arithmetic of
many pivots to matter, and there is no check of the tableau's
conditioning at all.
.PP
.B Minimise1
is checked on functions with one minimum in the interval given.
What it does when the interval holds two, or none, is described in
.IR optim (2)
and is not tested.
